/668dba9073658905cfbd208ef398f5d3f3cf0216b5f756007174dd24f51b21a9" :answer/id #uuid "dfa52140-e347-5780-80f6-be87c67e37fa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-253362/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c65aa76-4fd2-594e-bc83-d859473c1027" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-253362/field-3/668dba9073658905cfbd208ef398f5d3f3cf0216b5f756007174dd24f51b21a9" :answer-field/choices ["q-253362/field-3/0dccd1c0d01f1a6108a63b9727608bbe91e40a553b6f3a4aeaca05919f28c3b7" "q-253362/field-3/668dba9073658905cfbd208ef398f5d3f3cf0216b5f756007174dd24f51b21a9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-253362" :question/id #uuid "b1ec1408-7504-5b11-90fb-9d3bb0345917" :question/math-academy-id "q-253362" :question/problem "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2}+\\frac{{a}_{n-2}}{3}-4,\\,{a}_{1}=3,\\,{a}_{2}=2,\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2}+\\frac{{a}_{n-2}}{3}-4.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The previous terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ occur only in the first power and their  coefficients are constants. So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The relation is linear, and the forcing function $g(n)=-4$ is nonzero. So, the relation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$2,$\nis\n$\\text{linear},$\nand is\n$\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-253362/field-1" "q-253362/field-2" "q-253362/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f1c9d93-fcb3-51d2-bc82-badf49d45de7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-253362"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-265142/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "21c4eea8-ca79-528e-b7c3-ba2c46c56eb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-265142/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "f175f109-8d63-5205-9ff1-33c56944939a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-265142/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "14fbc438-3fbe-579b-8fda-0379c186fe64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-265142/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "298b4510-4f77-5e35-8e5d-931d0f61fc3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-265142/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-265142/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-265142/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-265142/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-265142/field-2/12092065ff4e11bb0d25fd69995cf910e9de428f4ac180ad1cb0a43a1ec62103" :answer/id #uuid "124dac3c-7ae2-5c7d-8a0e-53ce59bb196d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "linear" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-265142/field-2/54caa96b7e382d3213779913912fca1d2281930921d4a9e56c9cacfc55cd792f" :answer/id #uuid "9a292418-dfde-529d-95ff-5ba0f561b35d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "non-linear" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-265142/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "c0fa8cd6-07f0-57d2-a489-b54e4fa286d2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-265142/field-2/54caa96b7e382d3213779913912fca1d2281930921d4a9e56c9cacfc55cd792f" :answer-field/choices ["q-265142/field-2/12092065ff4e11bb0d25fd69995cf910e9de428f4ac180ad1cb0a43a1ec62103" "q-265142/field-2/54caa96b7e382d3213779913912fca1d2281930921d4a9e56c9cacfc55cd792f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-265142" :question/id #uuid "9d2fc3a8-ae2c-5fb1-941a-53137f621434" :question/math-academy-id "q-265142" :question/problem "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=3{a}_{n-1}+n({a}_{n-2})^{2}+5,\\,{a}_{1}=1,\\,{a}_{2}=2,\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}} and \nis\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}=3{a}_{n-1}+n({a}_{n-2})^{2}+5.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The previous term ${a}_{n-2}$ appears in the second power. So, the relation is non-linear.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$2$ and is\n$\\text{non-linear}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-265142/field-1" "q-265142/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f1c9d93-fcb3-51d2-bc82-badf49d45de7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-265142"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186126871 :answer-field/correct 17592186126874 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186126875 :answer-field/correct 17592186126877 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186126868 :answer-field/correct 17592186126870 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-253360"] :question/problem "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}-3{n}^{2},\\,{a}_{1}=10,\\,n\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}=2{a}_{n-1}-3{n}^{2}.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding term ${a}_{n-1}$ in the relation is $1.$ So, the relation has order $1.$\n\n- The previous term ${a}_{n-1}$ appears in the first power with constant  coefficient $2.$ So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The relation is linear, and the forcing function $g(n)=-3{n}^{2}$ is nonzero. So, the relation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$1,$\nis\n$\\text{linear},$\nand is\n$\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-253365/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "70360374-78bb-5947-9e1f-21eea9c50508" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-253365/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "520ed414-3d79-5b86-852e-9d758cdc56a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-253365/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "c4823a22-7188-549a-99e1-77770cc23d96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-253365/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf8afc7a-182e-502a-95c4-528b1b58aca2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-253365/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a7