08ef398f5d3f3cf0216b5f756007174dd24f51b21a9" :answer-field/choices ["q-253365/field-3/668dba9073658905cfbd208ef398f5d3f3cf0216b5f756007174dd24f51b21a9" "q-253365/field-3/0dccd1c0d01f1a6108a63b9727608bbe91e40a553b6f3a4aeaca05919f28c3b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-253365" :question/id #uuid "347005bc-29fd-55a3-8e95-980ad7df9f8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-253365" :question/problem "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}+2{a}_{n-2}+n,\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,{a}_{2}=1,\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}},\nis\n{{field-2}},\nand is\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}+2{a}_{n-2}+n.$ \n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The previous terms ${a}_{n-1}$  and ${a}_{n-2}$ occur only in the first power and their coefficients are constants. So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The relation is linear, and the forcing function $g(n)=n$ is nonzero. So, the relation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$2,$\nis\n$\\text{linear},$\nand is\n$\\text{inhomogeneous}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-253365/field-1" "q-253365/field-2" "q-253365/field-3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f1c9d93-fcb3-51d2-bc82-badf49d45de7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-253365"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-265152/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "e53ce2e6-a60e-5c5c-a3b4-4b0405b1724b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-265152/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "375a1658-bbb2-5636-bf00-60025931bb29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-265152/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "85239fda-c735-5857-b463-77ba076561d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-265152/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "700d12af-3d2b-5464-9e39-43e633fb44fb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-265152/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-265152/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-265152/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-265152/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-265152/field-2/12092065ff4e11bb0d25fd69995cf910e9de428f4ac180ad1cb0a43a1ec62103" :answer/id #uuid "06c8bb5a-6f36-5d5a-988d-664b2ab4be34" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "linear" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-265152/field-2/54caa96b7e382d3213779913912fca1d2281930921d4a9e56c9cacfc55cd792f" :answer/id #uuid "460a8993-abca-5ba6-a4d3-f6133ee6ed2a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "non-linear" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-265152/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "83a0bbf2-ca24-5cf0-922b-2ef5a634c9ff" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-265152/field-2/54caa96b7e382d3213779913912fca1d2281930921d4a9e56c9cacfc55cd792f" :answer-field/choices ["q-265152/field-2/12092065ff4e11bb0d25fd69995cf910e9de428f4ac180ad1cb0a43a1ec62103" "q-265152/field-2/54caa96b7e382d3213779913912fca1d2281930921d4a9e56c9cacfc55cd792f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-265152" :question/id #uuid "d88310da-b7f6-564c-a97c-433385879744" :question/math-academy-id "q-265152" :question/problem "Classify the recurrence relation\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}^{2}-2{a}_{n-2},\\,{a}_{1}=2,\\,{a}_{2}=1,\\,n\\ge 3.\n$$\n\nThe recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n{{field-1}} and\nis\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation ${a}_{n}$ is the largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses ${a}_{n}$ as a sum of previous terms ${a}_{n-1},…,{a}_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={s}_{1}{a}_{n-1}+{s}_{2}{a}_{n-2}+…+{s}_{k}{a}_{n-k}+g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere ${s}_{1},{s}_{2},…,{s}_{k}$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve ${a}_{n}$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation ${a}_{n}={a}_{n-1}^{2}-2{a}_{n-2}.$\n\n- The largest index difference between ${a}_{n}$ and the preceding terms ${a}_{n-1}$ and ${a}_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The term ${a}_{n-1}$ is not in the first power. So, the relation is non-linear.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation ${a}_{n}$ has order\n$2$ and is $\\text{non-linear}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-265152/field-1" "q-265152/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f1c9d93-fcb3-51d2-bc82-badf49d45de7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-265152"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-16539"] :question/problem "Find the fourth term of the sequence given by the recurrence relation\n\n$$\na_n = 5a_{n-1}+a_{n-2},\n\\qquad\na_1 = 2, \n\\quad\na_2 = 1, \n\\quad\nn \\geq 3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting with the initial terms $a_1 = 2,$ and $a_2 = 1,$ we compute the next terms using the recursive formula until we reach the fourth term.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =5{a}_{2}+{a}_{1}=5\\cdot 1+2=5+2=7 \\\\ {a}_{4} & =5{a}_{3}+{a}_{2}=5\\cdot 7+1=35+1=36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth term is $a_4 = 36.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-16541"] :question/problem "Which of the following linear recurrence relations are homogeneous?\n\n- $a_n = a_{n-1} + 5a_{n-2}+n$\n\n- $b_n = 3b_{n-1} -2b_{n-2}$\n\n- $c_n = 2c_{n-1} + 3c_{n-2}- c_{n-3}$" :question/worked-solution "A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is **homogeneous** if the forcing function is zero. \n\nIn other words, the linear recurrence relation\n\n$$\na_n = s_1a_{n-1} + s_2a_{n-2} + \\ldots + s_ka_{n-k} + g(n),\n$$\n\nis homogeneous if $g(n) = 0$ for all $n,$ and $s_i$ is constant for each $i.$\n\nLet's examine each recurrence relation in turn.\n\n- $a_n$ is not homogeneous. The term $n$ of the expression doesn't contain preceding terms of the relation.\n\n- $b_n$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $b_{n-1}$ and $b_{n-2}.$\n\n- $c_n$ is homogeneous. The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $c_{n-1},$ $c_{n-2},$ and $c_{n-3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-16555"] :question/problem "Fully classify the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\na_n =  a_{n-2} - \\dfrac23 a_{n-1},\n\\qquad\na_1 =0, \n\\quad\na_2 = 1,\n\\quad\nn \\geq 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following classifications of recurrence relations:\n\n- The order of a relation $a_n$ is the largest index difference between $a_n$ and the preceding terms in the relation.\n\n- A recurrence relation is *linear (with constant coefficients)* if it expresses $a_n$ as a sum of previous terms $a_{n-1}, \\dots, a_{n-k}$ with constant coefficients, together with possibly a final term that depends only on $n{:}$\n\n$$\na_n = s_{1}a_{n-1} + s_{2}a_{n-2} + \\ldots + s_{k}a_{n-k} + g(n)\n$$\n\nwhere $s_1, s_2, \\ldots, s_k$ are constants and $g(n)$ is a function, often called a *forcing function*, that does not involve $a_n$ or its preceding terms.\n\n- A linear recurrence relation (with constant coefficients) is *homogeneous* if the forcing function is zero. Otherwise, it is *inhomogeneous.*\n\nLet's classify the relation $a_n =  a_{n-2} - \\dfrac23 a_{n-1}.$\n\n- The largest index difference between $a_n$ and the preceding terms $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}$ in the relation is $2.$ So, the relation has order $2.$\n\n- The previous terms $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}$ occur only in the first power and their  coefficients are  constants. So, the relation is linear.\n\n- The terms of the expression each contain a preceding term, $a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n-2}.$ So, the relation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the recurrence relation $a_n$ has order\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2},$\nis\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{linear}},$\nand is\n$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{homogeneous}}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
